01:29:01,471 --> 01:29:06,191
More or less, programming languages can be divided into two groups.

562
01:29:06,871 --> 01:29:10,851
Interplated languages and compiled languages.

563
01:29:11,475 --> 01:29:18,295
When writing code in any programming language, first you write some source code.

564
01:29:19,355 --> 01:29:30,055
This source code is in a text format file that you can read and examine with basic tools such as NotPet or any world editors.

565
01:29:30,735 --> 01:29:39,475
It's understandable for humans, but it is not directly understandable and executable for computers.

566
01:29:39,475 --> 01:29:47,675
Therefore, we run a compiler and compile our source code into compiled code.

567
01:29:48,675 --> 01:30:02,375
In Java language, the extension of the source code is .java and the file extension of a compiled code is .class.

568
01:30:02,931 --> 01:30:09,731
And the compiler that we want is Java CXA.

569
01:30:11,192 --> 01:30:15,731
Of course, we use it when we don't use an IDE.

570
01:30:16,332 --> 01:30:22,731
It's much, much more easier and efficient when we use an IDE.

571
01:30:23,252 --> 01:30:26,152
But I have to tell you about those details.

572
01:30:26,676 --> 01:30:32,836
When we obtain compiled code, there are two alternative paths for programming languages.

573
01:30:33,996 --> 01:30:49,155
In compiled languages, a linker code tool is run, and compiled code has been transformed into executable code.

574
01:30:49,155 --> 01:30:53,315
This is directly executable by the target operating system.

575
01:30:53,844 --> 01:31:00,364
So, it has better runtime performance.

576
01:31:01,164 --> 01:31:18,284
However, the other branch is for interpreted languages, not operating system, but a program that is called interpreted takes this compiled code.

577
01:31:18,804 --> 01:31:27,264
Execute the statements within itself and tells the operating system what to do.

578
01:31:28,443 --> 01:31:35,443
Therefore, in interpreted languages, compiled code is not being run directly.

579
01:31:35,443 --> 01:31:37,884
It is being run indirectly.

580
01:31:38,663 --> 01:31:46,104
Therefore, there may be a small performance penalty in interpreted languages.

581
01:31:46,836 --> 01:32:11,956
But this performance penalty is being decreased every day. Moreover, if you are writing a real-world application, this application must communicate via internet, either a wire or a wireless network, and communicate with some server in the cloud, for example.

582
01:32:12,467 --> 01:32:17,867
And this communication gives us some delay.

583
01:32:18,487 --> 01:32:25,687
And this delay is much, much higher than the performance penalty of any interpreter.

584
01:32:28,427 --> 01:32:34,027
And interpretation has its own advantages too.

585
01:32:34,612 --> 01:32:42,331
First of all, as the code isn't being run directly, it is more secure.

586
01:32:43,791 --> 01:32:54,431
The interpreters, or in Java environment, we call it the Java virtual machine, virtual machine, executes the code.

587
01:32:54,431 --> 01:32:58,771
So, it will not allow some malicious code.

588
01:32:59,220 --> 01:33:11,600
To learn, to be in line directly in the operating system so that it becomes harder to use some security exploits of the operating system.

589
01:33:14,644 --> 01:33:18,684
This is an important advantage in terms of security.

590
01:33:19,403 --> 01:33:27,443
But in my opinion, the more important advantage is portability of your application.

591
01:33:29,224 --> 01:33:33,764
Java's official slogan is, right once, run anywhere.

592
01:33:34,703 --> 01:33:40,804
You can run a compiled code by using an interpreter or a Java virtual machine.

593
01:33:41,716 --> 01:33:47,756
In the same way in any hardware you can imagine.

594
01:33:49,036 --> 01:33:53,976
